# AGENTS.md — read this first

`mushketon-coach` — offline PWA for a shooting coach (ISSF 10 m air pistol).
The coach reads a shot position from the electronic target monitor and enters it manually:
tap on the target → drag to refine → live ISSF decimal score → release commits.
All data stays on the device (IndexedDB). No backend, no accounts, no sync.

## 1. Hard rules (never break)

1. NEVER commit unless the user explicitly says "commit" / "закоммить". Ask before committing.
2. NEVER deploy to GitHub Pages / production unless the user explicitly asks. Cloudflare Pages is
   the dev/staging environment used to check functionality; GitHub Pages via the `main` branch is
   production — these are separate targets, never conflate them or treat a Cloudflare deploy as a
   substitute for shipping to production. Cloudflare Pages is the exception: after tests/build pass
   for a change, `worker` deploys the current UI to the Cloudflare Pages project `musketoon-coach`
   automatically, without asking for separate confirmation, so the user can check the UI. Do not
   touch GitHub Actions or the GitHub Pages workflow to make this happen. If Cloudflare
   authorization or required env vars/secrets are missing, report the deploy error instead of
   silently skipping it or attempting a workaround. Every successful Cloudflare Pages deploy MUST
   be reported with the exact deployment URL (per-deploy hash URL) AND the permanent branch alias
   URL (`https://<branch>.musketoon-coach.pages.dev`), so the user always has a link to check. If
   the deploy did not run or failed, state that explicitly with the exact error — never omit the
   link silently or imply a deploy happened when it did not.
3. NEVER install/upgrade packages without explicit user approval.
4. NEVER add: backend API, user accounts, cloud DB (D1/KV), analytics, CDN/fonts from other
   origins, `unsafe-inline`/`unsafe-eval` in CSP, new runtime dependencies.
5. Change only what the task requires. Do not refactor or reformat adjacent code.
6. Keep scoring/geometry pure: `src/scoring.ts`, `src/transform.ts` must contain no React and no I/O.
7. Before any large change (multiple files/layers), write a plan in `docs/plans/PLAN-<feature>.md` and
   get approval — unless the user says "skip planning" / "just do it".
8. NEVER edit files in `main/`. `main/` is read-only reference; all work happens in a `wt-*/`
   worktree and reaches `main` only through merge. Direct edits in `main/` require an explicit
   one-off user request.
9. NEVER name concrete models, vendors, or agent-tool APIs in this file — describe roles and
   capabilities only. Model and cost bindings belong to the agent tool's own config.
10. When the user says "запомни эту информацию" / "запомни это поведение" (or an equivalent
    phrasing asking to remember something), record it as a permanent rule in this file
    (`AGENTS.md`): edit the working copy/source per the project's worktree rules in §3/§8
    (`wt-*/ai/AGENTS.md.settings`, never `main/` directly), do not commit it unless the user
    explicitly asks (see rule 1), and tell the user what was added/changed.

## 2. Commands

All commands run **inside a `wt-*/` worktree**, not in the container root and not in `main/`
(in `main/` read-only commands are allowed).

```bash
npm install
npm run dev        # Vite dev server
npx vitest run     # tests — must stay green (344 passing, 11 files)
npm run build      # tsc -p tsconfig.app.json + vite build → dist/
npm run preview
```

`npm test` also works. After code changes always run `npx vitest run`; run `npm run build` too if
types could break. There is no local deploy script: pushing `main` triggers
`.github/workflows/deploy-github-pages.yml` (production, explicit user request only). Deploying to
Cloudflare Pages (dev/staging, via `wrangler pages deploy dist --project-name musketoon-coach`)
happens automatically after a successful test/build for a change, without a separate user request,
so the user can check the UI; it stays separate from the production GitHub Pages pipeline and does
not involve editing GitHub Actions. If Cloudflare auth or config is missing, report the failure.

Test conventions:

- `vitest.config.ts` sets `globals: false` — always import `describe/it/expect` from `vitest`.
- Component tests render with `renderToStaticMarkup` (no DOM environment), e.g.
  `targetCanvas.render.test.tsx`, `Modal.test.tsx`.
- IndexedDB tests start with `import 'fake-indexeddb/auto';`.
- `npm run build` type-checks via `tsconfig.app.json`, which does not cover test files.

## 3. Where things are

```
src/scoring.ts        ISSF decimal score (pure)          + scoring.test.ts
src/transform.ts      screen ↔ target coordinates (pure) + transform.test.ts
src/db/               IndexedDB: schema, open, tx+epoch, settings, startup cleanup
src/domain/           athleteRepo, trainingRepo, shotRepo, commentRepo, backupService, index.ts
src/components/       TargetCanvas (SVG target + shots), TargetLoupe, Modal + modalController,
                      UpdateBanner, shotMarker, targetLabels
src/screens/          AthletesScreen, TrainingsScreen, TrainingScreen, RemarksScreen,
                      SettingsScreen
src/utils/            useWakeLock + wakeLockController
src/styles/           tokens.css (design tokens), reset.css, common.module.css
src/App.tsx           screen routing        src/main.tsx bootstrap      src/sw.ts service worker
public/_headers       CSP + security headers
scripts/gen-icons.py  regenerates PWA icons
vite.config.ts        Vite + vite-plugin-pwa (injectManifest, prompt update); base is
                      `/mushketon-coach/` for GitHub Pages, `/` otherwise
.github/workflows/deploy-github-pages.yml   build + deploy on push to main (production hosting)
wrangler.toml         Cloudflare Pages (dev/staging hosting for checking functionality, output dir dist)
docs/plans/PLAN.md         full spec, source of truth (RU)
docs/plans/PLAN-*.md       per-feature plans; use docs/plans/PLAN-TARGET-ZOOM.md as template
docs/specs/, docs/bugs/    bugfix specs and bug reports
docs/reference/CHANGES-TARGET-VISUAL.md  exact ISSF target geometry and colors
docs/reference/CLOUDFLARE-FREE-CONSTRAINTS.md  limits of the free hosting tier
```

All paths above (`src/…`, `docs/plans/…`, etc.) are relative to the **current worktree**, e.g.
`main/src/scoring.ts`. Container layout (see §9 Worktrees):

```
mushketon-coach/        # container, path never changes — the agent starts here
├── .bare/               # bare git dir
├── .git                 # pointer file: "gitdir: ./.bare"
├── .pi/ .claude/ .agents/               # agent-tool config — root only, never in a worktree
├── .wrangler/ node_modules/            # real here; node_modules is symlinked into each worktree
│                                     # by a committed symlink, .wrangler only on demand
├── main/                # worktree of branch main — read-only, never edited directly
└── wt-<feature>/        # feature worktrees, created on demand
```

The agent tool is started from the container root, so its config (`.pi/`, `.claude/`, `.agents/`)
lives there only — a worktree must never have its own copy or symlink of it, it should contain code
only (plus the build/deploy symlinks it needs).

`AGENTS.md` in the container root is a symlink to `main/ai/AGENTS.md.settings`. Edit these rules
only in `wt-*/ai/AGENTS.md.settings`; the root file updates itself after the merge into `main`.
Until that merge, the worktree and the root can show different versions of the rules.

Stack: TypeScript, React 18, Vite 5, vite-plugin-pwa, raw IndexedDB, Vitest + fake-indexeddb.

## 4. Data conventions

- Coordinates: integer hundredths of mm (`xh`, `yh`).
- Score: integer tenths (109..10, or 0 for miss); displayed as ISSF decimal 10.0–10.9.
- Shot status: `draft` | `committed`.
- `TrainingRecord.nextShotNumber` is monotonic — shot numbers are event ordinals, NEVER renumbered.
- `dataEpoch` guards writes from stale clients after a restore.
- Backup = one JSON file (`version: 1`) with all data; see `BackupFile` in
  `src/domain/backupService.ts`. Restore is validated and atomic. Backup strings render as text only.
- Styling: CSS Modules next to the component (`Foo.module.css`); take colors and spacing from
  `src/styles/tokens.css`, do not hardcode them.

## 5. Product decisions (locked)

- Phone-first, portrait-first PWA; tablet secondary; offline-first.
- Coach must select/create an athlete before anything else; several athletes at once with fast
  switching that must not reset the current training.
- Training = sequence of N shots, no fixed series size; can be reopened and continued.
- All hits are drawn on target, older ones less prominent.
- Last shot and older shots are editable repeatedly; undo exists for the last created shot.
- Deletion requires confirmation.
- Shot comments (remarks) are implemented: `commentRepo` + `RemarksScreen`, comments link to their
  training shots.
- AI analysis is implemented: `SettingsScreen` → «Анализ с AI» section, OpenRouter OAuth PKCE
  login, dynamic free-model list, multi-training selection, JSON prompt with all shot data and
  comments, response shown in a modal. See `docs/plans/PLAN-AI-ANALYSIS.md`.

Not in MVP: charts, group stats, sighting/match series, sync, accounts, cloud DB,
electronic-target integration, camera recognition, competitions, per-training export,
AI analysis history, streaming AI response.

## 6. Status

Done: scoring, transform, IndexedDB layer, domain repos, all five screens, shot remarks,
backup/restore, PWA, CSP, GitHub Pages deploy workflow, Cloudflare Pages config,
AI analysis (OpenRouter OAuth, free-model list, multi-training select, JSON prompt, modal response). Tests green (587).
Partial: ISSF target visuals — verify against `docs/reference/CHANGES-TARGET-VISUAL.md` before touching.
Missing: athlete switcher inside the training screen (`docs/plans/PLAN.md` §6).

## 7. Delegating to helper agents

Roles, cheapest first: `scout` < `delegate` < `researcher` < `worker` < `reviewer` < `oracle`.
Model and cost bindings per role live in the agent tool's own config, never in this file.

| Task | Agent |
| --- | --- |
| Fast local recon: relevant files, entry points, data flow, risks | `scout` (read-only) |
| External facts: web/docs research, brief with sources; never repo facts (that is `scout`) | `researcher` (read-only) |
| Write code, edit files, run tests/build, deploy | `worker` (only agent that edits) |
| Check worker's result vs task/plan, tests, edge cases, simplicity; report only | `reviewer` (read-only) |
| Second opinion on a risky decision, or worker failed twice | `oracle` (advisory, never edits) |
| Small self-contained chore needing no special role (summarize a doc, draft a plan skeleton) | `delegate` (read-only) |

`delegate` is never used for code edits, deploys, or as a replacement for `reviewer`.

The builtin `reviewer` can make small fixes by default; this project overrides that — `reviewer`
never edits here, every fix goes back to `worker`.

Default pipeline: `scout` (+ `researcher` when external facts matter) → plan → `worker` →
`reviewer`, with `oracle` inserted before acting when the decision itself is risky.

Rule of thumb: `scout` before you understand the code, `researcher` before you trust external
facts, `worker` to implement, `reviewer` to check, `oracle` when the decision feels risky,
`delegate` for trivia.

Read-only agents (`scout`/`researcher`/`reviewer`/`oracle`/`delegate`) may run in `main/` for
read-only inspection; write work is `worker`-only and happens only in a `wt-*/` worktree (see §8).

A documentation-lookup capability for current library/framework docs (React, Vite, vite-plugin-pwa,
Vitest, Wrangler) is granted to `researcher`, `worker`, `reviewer` and `oracle`, and not to `scout`
or `delegate`, so they do not rely on stale model knowledge. Use it for API/config facts about
third-party tools; repo facts still come from `scout`. The grant itself lives in the agent tool's
own config, not in this file (see rule 9 in §1).

Rules:
- The coordinator **never** edits code and **never** deploys — it always delegates to `worker` and
  never falls back to doing the work itself. `worker` is the only role that changes code or deploys.
- `reviewer` is **mandatory** after every `worker` change — no exceptions for size or triviality
  (including docs, comments, formatting). It only reports findings and never edits files; every fix
  goes back to `worker`, which re-verifies and is then re-reviewed.
- `worker` always runs inside a `wt-*/` worktree, never in `main/`. One writer per worktree;
  parallel work needs separate worktrees (see §8).
- Every implementation handoff starts from a clean context (no inherited chat history) and carries
  one focused task + plan reference + acceptance criteria + verification command (usually
  `npx vitest run`).
- If `worker` fails (tests/build/deploy), the coordinator fixes the handoff or the environment and
  re-runs `worker` — it does not fix the code itself.
- Deploy and commit: only `worker`. Committing and deploying to GitHub Pages `main` (production)
  require explicit user permission (see §1). Deploying to Cloudflare Pages (dev/staging) happens
  automatically after a successful test/build, without waiting for a separate user request or for
  `reviewer`. Cloudflare and GitHub Pages `main` are separate targets — deploying to one is never a
  substitute for or implicitly authorized by a request to deploy to the other.
- Never use `oracle` for recon or routine review; never use `worker` for read-only questions;
  never use `researcher` for repo recon; never use `delegate` for code changes or review.

## 8. Worktrees

Create a feature worktree:

```bash
cd ~/Downloads/home/mushketon-coach
git worktree add wt-<feature> -b <feature>
cd wt-<feature> && npx vitest run
```

`node_modules` is committed as a relative symlink (`../node_modules`, git mode 120000, commit
`15d6692`), so `git worktree add` materializes it automatically — do not create it by hand.
`.env` and `.wrangler` are gitignored and stay in the container root only; symlink them into a
worktree by hand if a task actually needs build/deploy state.

Remove it: `git worktree remove wt-<feature>` (symlinks go with the folder).

Rules:
- `main/` is never a work directory: no edits, no `worker` runs there.
- `docs/plans/` and code are versioned per branch, same as before.
- `.pi`, `.claude`, `.agents` stay **only at the container root** — agent-tool config, not needed
  inside a worktree. Do not symlink or copy them into `main/` or `wt-*/`.
- If a branch needs different dependencies, replace the `node_modules` symlink with a real local
  `npm install` in that worktree.
- One `worker` writer per worktree. Parallel tasks → separate worktrees (see §7).
